<title>UK: GEC-Alsthom penalised for late delivery-FT.</title>
<headline>GEC-Alsthom penalised for late delivery-FT.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-01-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Anglo-French railway engineering group GEC-Alsthom paid several million pounds in penalties to London Transport because of a 12-month delay in delivering new trains, the Financial Times reported on Monday.</p>
<p>The trains are part of the modernisation of London Underground's Northern Line.</p>
<p>The Financial Times said the contract, worth between 800-900 million pounds, required GEC-Alsthom to supply 100 trains and maintain them for 20 years, with an option for London Underground to extend this to 36 years.</p>
<p>The newspaper did not give an exact amount for the penalties.</p>
<p>--London Newsroom +44 171 542 6784</p>
